        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(media): make fetch retry backoff abortable with caller signal",
        "body": "## Summary\nMake the retry backoff in media fetch operations abortable so caller cancellation interrupts the delay between fetch attempts immediately.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\nWhen media fetch retry is enabled (`options.retry`), `withMediaFetchRetry` calls `retryAsync` without a custom `sleep` function. The default sleep uses `setTimeout` which is not interruptible by an abort signal. If the caller has an abort context (e.g., tool execution cancelled, timeout fired), the retry backoff delay runs to completion before the abort can take effect.\n\n**Code evidence**: In `src/media/fetch.ts:548`, `retryAsync` is called without a `sleep` option. `FetchMediaOptions` has `timeoutMs`, `responseHeaderTimeoutMs`, and `readIdleTimeoutMs` for per-request timeouts, but no signal for the inter-retry backoff.\n\n## Root Cause\n`retryAsync` defaults to `setTimeout(resolve, ms)` for its backoff sleep. Without passing a custom `sleep` that uses `sleepWithAbort(ms, signal)`, the retry backoff is not abortable. The `FetchMediaOptions` type has no `signal` field, so callers cannot thread an abort signal through to the retry backoff.\n\n## Why This Fix\nAdd an optional `signal?: AbortSignal` field to `FetchMediaOptions` and use it in `withMediaFetchRetry` to provide an abortable `sleep` when available. The signal is placed before `...retry` in the options spread so that an explicit `retry.sleep` takes precedence, preserving backward compatibility.\n\nThis follows the identical pattern as:\n- PR #109913: `fix(discord): honor caller abortSignal during 429 retry backoff`\n- PR #109805: `fix(auto-reply): honor abortSignal in transient-HTTP retry backoff`\n- PR #110427: `fix(compaction): make retry backoff abortable with caller signal`\n\n## User Impact\n- **Before**: Cancelling a media fetch during retry backoff would wait for the full backoff delay before checking for cancellation.\n- **After**: When callers pass `signal` on `FetchMediaOptions`, cancellation interrupts the retry backoff immediately. No behavior change when signal is omitted.\n\n## Evidence\n- Wall-clock proof demonstrates the mechanism: `retryAsync` without `sleepWithAbort` takes 801ms to notice an abort fired at 50ms; with `sleepWithAbort`, it notices in ~51ms with only 1 fetch attempt instead of 2.\n- The `signal` field is placed before `...retry` in the spread, so an explicit `retry.sleep` always takes precedence — backward-compatible by design.\n- `sleepWithAbort` is well-tested in `@openclaw/retry` and used by 10+ call sites across the codebase (auto-reply, discord, compaction, worker, etc.).\n\n## Real Behavior Proof\n\n### Before (bare setTimeout, no signal)\n```bash\n$ node --import tsx scripts/proof-media-fetch-retry-abort.mjs\n=== BEFORE (bare setTimeout, no signal) ===\n{\n  \"elapsedMs\": 801,\n  \"calls\": 2,\n  \"verdict\": \"FAIL: abort did not interrupt backoff\"\n}\n```\nAbort fired at ~50ms; the retry still waited the full 800ms backoff and attempted a 2nd fetch call.\n\n### After (sleepWithAbort via options.signal)\n```bash\n=== AFTER (sleepWithAbort via options.signal) ===\n{\n  \"elapsedMs\": 51,\n  \"calls\": 1,\n  \"verdict\": \"PASS: abort interrupted backoff immediately\"\n}\n```\nSame abort timing; the backoff was interrupted in ~1ms and no retry was attempted.\n\n## Tests and Validation\n- `pnpm check` passed\n- `pnpm test -- src/media/fetch.test.ts` — 47/47 passed (includes new regression test)\n- New regression test: \"aborts promptly during retry backoff when the caller signal fires\" — verifies abort interrupts the 500ms retry delay with fake timers, confirms exactly 1 call and 0 remaining timers\n- Existing media fetch tests unchanged — the optional `signal` field is backward-compatible\n",
        "branch": "fix/media-fetch-retry-abortable-backoff",
